tration and funding"],"sponsorBioguideId":"G000568","cosponsorCount":59,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/27","industries":[{"industry":"pharma","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The bill changes registration and licensing requirements for pharmaceutical researchers working with controlled substances and affects the regulatory classification of fentanyl-related compounds."},{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"The bill establishes new criminal penalties and quantity thresholds that will affect defense attorneys and prosecutors handling drug trafficking cases."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would permanently classify all fentanyl-related substances as Schedule I controlled substances, meaning they would be treated as having high abuse potential and no accepted medical use. It sets the same criminal penalties and quantity thresholds for fentanyl-related substances as currently apply to fentanyl analogues. The bill also creates a streamlined registration process for researchers studying Schedule I substances and makes it easier for registered researchers to conduct certain activities without additional permits.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"People caught up in the criminal law, and victims of crime.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"H001086","score":0.08496351694573398},"overlapCount":58},"meaning":{"percentile":83.97223536835463,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.6610394937457147}},{"billId":"119-hr-685","bioguideId":"M001194","cycle":2026,"score":0.021090237584737992,"matches":[{"industry":"pharma","donorAmount":23885,"donorShare":0.02063534610767091,"billConfidence":0.95,"contribution":0.01089087711238187},{"industry":"health-providers","donorAmount":25000,"donorShare":0.021598645706165907,"billConfidence":0.85,"contribution":0.010199360472356124}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.6610394937457147},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-685","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"685","title":"SAVE Moms and Babies Act of 2025","introducedDate":"2025-01-23","latestActionDate":"2025-01-23","latestActionText":"Referred to the House Committee on Energy and Commerce.","policyArea":"Health","subjects":["Abortion","Drug safety, medical device, and laboratory regulation","Health information and medical records","Health personnel","Health technology, devices, supplies","Marketing and advertising","Prescription drugs"],"sponsorBioguideId":"L000566","cosponsorCount":62,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/685","industries":[{"industry":"pharma","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"The bill directly prohibits approval of new abortion drugs and imposes significant new regulatory restrictions on already-approved drugs in this category."},{"industry":"health-providers","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The bill creates new certification requirements for prescribing practitioners and mandates they have surgical intervention capability, changing practice obligations."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would prohibit the FDA from approving any new drugs intended to terminate pregnancy. For drugs already approved for this purpose, it would impose new restrictions: requiring in-person dispensing by a specially certified practitioner, prohibiting use after 70 days of gestation, and mandating that the practitioner be able to provide surgical intervention. The bill would also prevent practitioners from both prescribing and dispensing these medications.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who fills a prescription. Anyone who takes a medicine.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"H001086","score":0.11251216543663692},"overlapCount":62},"meaning":{"percentile":76.4631645369932,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.6610394937457147}},{"billId":"119-hr-7","bioguideId":"M001194","cycle":2026,"score":0.014942790105239544,"matches":[{"industry":"health-providers","donorAmount":25000,"donorShare":0.021598645706165907,"billConfidence":0.85,"contribution":0.00719954856872197},{"industry":"insurance","donorAmount":20505,"donorShare":0.017715209208197278,"billConfidence":0.95,"contribution":0.006599783822661731},{"industry":"ideological-single-issue","donorAmount":4500,"donorShare":0.0038877562271098635,"billConfidence":0.75,"contribution":0.0011434577138558423}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.6610394937457147},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-7","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"7","title":"No Taxpayer Funding for Abortion and Abortion Insurance Full Disclosure Act of 2025","introducedDate":"2025-01-22","latestActionDate":"2025-01-22","latestActionText":"Referred to the Committee on Energy and Commerce, and in addition to the Committees on the Judiciary, and Ways and Means, for a period to be subsequently determined by the Speaker, in each case for consideration of such provisions as fall within the jurisdiction of the committee concerned.","policyArea":"Health","subjects":["Abortion","Appropriations","Assault and harassment offenses","Comprehensive health care","Consumer affairs","Crimes against women","Employee benefits and pensions","Government buildings, facilities, and property","Health care costs and insurance","Health care coverage and access","Health facilities and institutions","Health programs administration and funding"],"sponsorBioguideId":"S000522","cosponsorCount":109,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/7","industries":[{"industry":"insurance","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"The bill directly restricts what health insurance plans may cover and how federal subsidies interact with abortion coverage in qualified health plans."},{"industry":"health-providers","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The bill prohibits federal health care facilities and federal employees from providing abortion services, affecting facilities that currently receive federal funds."},{"industry":"ideological-single-issue","confidence":0.75,"rationale":"Organizations focused on reproductive health policy and abortion access or restrictions have a direct stake in the regulatory framework this bill establishes."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would make permanent the restrictions on federal funding for abortion services and health insurance coverage that includes abortion. Currently, these restrictions are renewed each year through appropriations bills; the bill would apply them across all federal programs and funds. It would also prohibit qualified health plans offered through insurance marketplaces from covering abortion. The bill includes exceptions for cases of rape, incest, or when the pregnant person's life is in danger.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who buys health insurance. Anyone who pays a medical bill.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"B001260","score":0.061260165720508956},"overlapCount":107},"meaning":{"percentile":63.03833412210128,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.6610394937457147}}],"votes":[{"id":"a64a90b759595d68f3a8d502","billId":"119-hr-7008","date":"2026-07-22T16:51:00-04:00","question":"Yea-and-Nay","result":"Passed","position":"Yea","sourceUrl":"https://clerk.house.gov/evs/2026/roll280.xml"},{"id":"2afa499bcaceefdcecfa862a","